     "data": {
      "alt": "Swedish prime minister Ulf Kristersson of the Moderate Party leaves the official residence, Sager House, a day after the Swedish general election in Stockholm, Sweden.",
      "caption": "Swedish prime minister Ulf Kristersson of the Moderate Party leaves the official residence, Sager House, a day after the Swedish general election in Stockholm, Sweden.",
      "credit": "Photograph: Claudio Bresciani/EPA"
     }
    },
    {
     "_type": "model.dotcomrendering.pageElements.TextBlockElement",
     "html": "<p>He says there are some conversations scheduled in the meantime – including with fellow coalition leaders this evening – but he essentially wants to see the final results show first.</p>",
     "elementId": "f652eb8d-e5e8-4b1d-a488-600fb3bc5df7"
    },
    {
     "_type": "model.dotcomrendering.pageElements.TextBlockElement",
     "html": "<p>He also <strong>repeats his last night’s suggestion of exploring alternative majorities</strong> in the parliament.</p>",
     "elementId": "848b0258-5caf-407e-a746-90e2176677ad"
    },
    {
     "_type": "model.dotcomrendering.pageElements.TextBlockElement",
     "html": "<p>One possible option could involve the Centre party switching sides, but it’s not immediately obvious how Kristersson would get them to accept a government with the far-right <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/sweden\" data-component=\"auto-linked-tag\">Sweden</a> Democrats with it.</p>",
     "elementId": "2b3cca69-2e77-4f0d-aea8-5e395618bdfc"
    }
   ],
